<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net9.0-android</TargetFramework>
		<UseMaui>true</UseMaui>
		<SupportedOSPlatformVersion>26</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<!-- NuGet Package Properties -->
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>false</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
		<PackageId>BaluMediaServer.CameraStreamer</PackageId>
		<PackageVersion>1.5.32</PackageVersion>
		<Authors>Nakano Miku 13</Authors>
		<Company>Balu Media Server</Company>
		<Product>Balu Media Camera Streamer</Product>
		<Description>Android camera streaming library with dual camera support and optional AAC audio for MAUI applications</Description>
		<Copyright>Copyright © Balu Media Server 2025</Copyright>
		<PackageTags>android;camera;streaming;maui;dual-camera;rtsp;h264;mjpeg;rtp;rtcp;video;aac;audio;h265;RTSP;rtsp</PackageTags>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/BaluMediaServer</PackageProjectUrl>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/BaluMediaServer/BaluMediaServer</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>v1.5.32: Live audio — the AAC-LC pipeline is now fully wired end-to-end. When ServerConfiguration.EnableAudioTrack is true, the microphone is captured (AudioCaptureService, PCM-16, 1024 samples/AAC access unit), hardware-encoded to AAC-LC (AacEncoder, audio/mp4a-latm, raw access units, no ADTS) via a shared AacEncoderManager, and emitted as live RTP on the second m=audio track (trackID=1) packetised per RFC 3640 mpeg4-generic AAC-hbr — each access unit gets its own SSRC/sequence/timestamp space, isolated from the video. The audio pipeline starts on the first audio-track SETUP and is torn down when the last client leaves or the server stops. Earlier builds advertised the m=audio track in the SDP and accepted SETUP for trackID=1 but did not emit audio RTP (SDP/SETUP compatibility path only); v1.5.32 completes it so audio actually plays in VLC/FFmpeg/ONVIF clients. Sample rate and channel count come from AudioSampleRateHz/AudioChannels; off by default so single-track clients see byte-identical SDP. Requires android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO in the host manifest plus a runtime grant; if the grant is missing the video pipeline keeps working and AudioCaptureService raises ErrorOccurred instead of crashing. Documentation-only release: no media-pipeline behavior changed beyond the version bump. Still on the roadmap: RTCP Sender Reports for the audio track and explicit A/V lip-sync hardening. (Also folds in the v1.5.31 work: runtime camera switching with STOP_CAMERA_*/SWITCH_CAMERA fully implemented, and LAN-aware IP selection preferring a reachable LAN interface over a VPN tunnel.)

v1.5.31: ONVIF Profile S + LAN-aware discovery, software stabilization, and runtime camera switching. (1) ONVIF Profile S (opt-in via ServerConfiguration.Onvif, default disabled): the device auto-discovers into any VMS/NVR/ONVIF client. WS-Discovery announces it on the LAN (Hello/Bye + ProbeMatch on 239.255.255.250:3702); a Device + Media SOAP service (HttpListener, default port 8090) answers GetDeviceInformation/GetCapabilities/GetServices/GetScopes and GetProfiles/GetStreamUri/GetSnapshotUri/GetVideoEncoderConfigurations. GetStreamUri returns the existing RTSP URL, GetSnapshotUri returns a cached JPEG (/snapshot/back.jpg|front.jpg on the MJPEG port). Auth is WS-UsernameToken PasswordDigest against the existing RTSP user store. All SOAP/XML/digest/discovery logic is Android-free and unit-tested. Scope is Profile S only (no PTZ/Events/Imaging). (2) LAN-aware IP selection: the advertised host (RTSP SDP c= line + ONVIF XAddr/GetStreamUri/GetSnapshotUri/WS-Discovery) prefers a directly-reachable LAN interface and only falls back to a VPN tunnel when no LAN address exists. Interfaces are classified by NAME (wlan*/eth*/rmnet* vs tun*/tap*/ppp*/wg*) because Mono-on-Android reports the Wi-Fi NIC type as Unknown; loopback and link-local are excluded. Fixes a Tailscale/WireGuard tunnel IP being advertised to a LAN VMS. (3) Software noise-reduction + digital stabilization (opt-in via ServerConfiguration.BackCameraStabilization, back camera only, default off): motion-adaptive temporal denoise (also lowers bitrate) + translation-only digital stabilization, applied to NV21 before H.264 encoding. Self-times per frame and permanently self-disables if it runs over budget, so it can never silently regress framerate; kernels (FrameStabilizerMath) are Android-free and unit-tested. (4) Runtime camera switching: new Server.SwitchCameras() and Server.SetCameraActive(bool front, bool start) start/stop a single camera without tearing down the server, for devices that cannot run both lenses at once. BussCommand.STOP_CAMERA_*/SWITCH_CAMERA are now fully implemented (STOP_CAMERA_* actually releases the camera); MjpegServer.Stop() no longer tears down shared cameras. Verified live on a MediaTek device running Tailscale (WS-Discovery, full Device/Media SOAP, RTSP H.264 1080p + AAC, all reporting the reachable LAN address).

v1.5.30: Stall resilience — a stalled camera no longer drops the client. While the camera delivers no frames, the server feeds a synthetic dark "STREAM STALLED" placeholder (black frame + message + live elapsed-seconds timer) into the H.264/MJPEG streams so the session stays open, and real video resumes seamlessly on the same session once the watchdog re-opens the camera. A camera-stall vs encoder-stall distinction restarts just a hung encoder (~1s) instead of the whole camera (10-30s on MediaTek). Opt-out via ServerConfiguration.StallPlaceholderEnabled (default true).

v1.5.29: High-resolution pipeline performance + quality. Bitrate auto-scaled to resolution (~0.2 bits/pixel/frame, e.g. 1920x1080@30 → ~12.4 Mbps) instead of the old hardcoded 2 Mbps, with a per-camera manual override honored verbatim (Server.SetBack/FrontCameraBitrate(int), Set*AutoBitrate(), Get*Bitrate(), static GetRecommendedBitrate(w,h)). RTCP adaptive bitrate no longer drags the encoder below its configured value — the configured bitrate is the adaptation ceiling and ServerConfiguration.AdaptiveBitrate / Server.SetAdaptiveBitrate(false) pins it. CRITICAL: camera SENSOR_TIMESTAMP (ns) is now converted to µs before MediaCodec — raw ns made the MediaTek C2 time-based GOP logic force an IDR on every frame (~190KB/frame, ~7-11fps at 2K). Camera frames marshalled into a reusable 6-slot ring via JNIEnv.CopyArray (NOTE: FrameEventArgs.Data is reused after ~6 frames — subscribers must copy if they retain it). KeyLatency=0 removed to restore hardware encoder pipelining; NV21→NV12 chroma swap vectorized (SIMD); encoder raised 25→30fps; H.264 Main profile used when supported.

v1.5.28: Optional AAC audio streaming — a second m=audio RTSP track (trackID=1) carrying hardware-encoded AAC-LC alongside the H.264 video track. New ServerConfiguration flags: EnableAudioTrack (default false — fully backward compatible), AudioSampleRateHz (44100), AudioChannels (1). SDP advertises mpeg4-generic with RFC 3640 AAC-hbr fmtp; SETUP for trackID=1 gets its own SSRC/sequence and independent RTP stream. The audio pipeline starts on the first audio-track SETUP and is torn down when the last client leaves. Verified with VLC and ffplay multi-track SDP parsing on net9.0-android.</PackageReleaseNotes>
		
		<!-- Include symbols for debugging -->
		<IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
		<SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
		
		<!-- Include dependencies -->
		<DevelopmentDependency>false</DevelopmentDependency>
	</PropertyGroup>


	<!-- Exclude test project folder from compilation -->
	<ItemGroup>
		<Compile Remove="BaluMediaServer.Tests\**" />
		<None Remove="BaluMediaServer.Tests\**" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Maui.Essentials" Version="$(MauiVersion)" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Maui.Controls" Version="9.0.51" />
		<!-- Pin to match the version resolved by Microsoft.Maui.Controls, silencing NU1608 -->
		<PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.Collection.Ktx" Version="1.5.0.1" />
	</ItemGroup>
	<!-- Pack the README into the package root so nuget.org renders it on the package page -->
	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<AndroidLibrary Include="Jar\camerastreamer-release.aar" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<TransformFile Include="Transforms\Metadata.xml" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- Make BaluLogger available in every file without an explicit using statement -->
	<ItemGroup>
		<Using Include="BaluMediaServer" />
	</ItemGroup>
</Project>
